<h2>The dispute in outline</h2>
<p>The parties built a layered commercial relationship: a licence agreement in January 2019 for technical collaboration, a joint venture agreement in October 2021 that created an Indian joint venture company, and an MoU in February 2023 for liaison and logistics services on a Mumbai project. Each layer carried its own arbitration clause — Seoul under Korean law for the licence agreement, Mumbai under Indian law for the JVA and the MoU. When the Indian petitioners alleged breach of the JVA's non-compete covenant through the incorporation of a rival subsidiary, and claimed about Rs. 4.24 crore under the MoU along with larger business losses, they invoked the two Mumbai clauses. The respondents answered that the real dispute belonged to the licence agreement — and therefore to Seoul, where a Korean arbitration over exclusivity was already running.</p>

<h2>The narrow gate of Section 11(6-A)</h2>
<p>The Court's starting point was the statutory text: on a Section 11 application, the referral court confines itself to the examination of the existence of an arbitration agreement. Once a prima facie arbitration agreement is shown, the court ought to appoint an arbitrator and refer the parties to arbitration. Everything else — the true scope of each clause, which claims belong where, whether the pre-arbitral settlement steps were satisfied, whether a non-signatory is bound — belongs to the tribunal under the competence-competence principle.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Different fields</strong><p>The JVA, the MoU and the licence agreement prima facie operate in different fields; disputes arising exclusively under the first two fall to their own clauses.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Overlap is not a bar</strong><p>That some claims may brush against the licence agreement does not defeat the reference; demarcation is the arbitrator's task.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Non-signatory</strong><p>Whether the respondent's wholly owned subsidiary — allegedly incorporated in breach of the non-compete — is bound was left to the tribunal.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Appointment</strong><p>A former Chief Justice of the Bombay High Court was appointed sole arbitrator for the JVA and MoU disputes, free to fix his own fees.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why the decision matters for drafting and strategy</h2>
<p>Multi-contract relationships with mismatched dispute resolution clauses are now the norm in cross-border collaborations, and this judgment confirms the Indian position at the appointment stage: each arbitration clause is taken on its own terms, and a party cannot use an earlier foreign-seated clause as a jurisdictional veto over later India-seated agreements. The tactical consequence runs both ways. A claimant can frame claims by reference to the specific agreement whose clause suits it, knowing that the referral court will not conduct a mini-trial on which contract "really" governs. A respondent, conversely, must save its overlap, settlement-procedure and non-signatory objections for the tribunal, where they remain fully open — a Section 11 reference decides existence, not scope.</p>

<h2>The practical checklist</h2>
<p>For businesses negotiating related agreements, the lesson is to align dispute resolution clauses deliberately — same seat, same rules, ideally a consolidation mechanism — or to accept that parallel proceedings in different seats are a real possibility. For counsel resisting a Section 11 petition, the decision narrows the field to genuine non-existence arguments: no concluded agreement, no arbitration clause covering the parties at all, or a clearly non-arbitrable subject. Objections that require construing the commercial relationship across several contracts will almost always be deferred to the arbitrator.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. The arbitral proceedings directed by the judgment will determine the parties' rights; nothing here is an opinion on the merits of those disputes.</p>
</div>
